WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.700
SPEAKER 1: They
point to the presence

00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.640
of America in the
world's imagination,

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:08.475
even before America was settled.

00:00:08.475 --> 00:00:10.191
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:19.060
So where do we begin this story?

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:22.150
History always begins
in the middle of things.

00:00:22.150 --> 00:00:25.570
The Western hemisphere in
which we Americans live

00:00:25.570 --> 00:00:30.220
was inhabited by people who
came from elsewhere, unwilling,

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:33.130
for the most part, to settle
for the conditions in which they

00:00:33.130 --> 00:00:37.810
were born and drawn by the
possibility of a new life

00:00:37.810 --> 00:00:39.380
or a new chance at life.

00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:43.900
The lure of freedom, the space
to pursue their ambitions

00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:47.230
in the way that whatever
old world they came from

00:00:47.230 --> 00:00:49.180
did not permit.

00:00:49.180 --> 00:00:52.660
The first settlers came
across the Bering Straits

00:00:52.660 --> 00:00:57.250
from Northeastern
Asia and filtered down

00:00:57.250 --> 00:01:01.060
into the settling of the
continent from the Yukon

00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:06.250
down to Tierra Del
Fuego and South America.

00:01:06.250 --> 00:01:11.020
We have the evidence of peoples
who have vanished since.

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:16.510
The Inca, the Maya, the
Aztecs, the grand civilizations

00:01:16.510 --> 00:01:21.860
that flourished and flamed
out and died in various ways,

00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:29.730
leaving behind splendid remnants
of their lives and livelihood.

00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:32.750
We see in the North American
continent earthworks

00:01:32.750 --> 00:01:33.530
and mounds--

00:01:33.530 --> 00:01:34.880
burial mounds.

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:37.370
They're somewhat
mysterious, but that

00:01:37.370 --> 00:01:41.180
are the remnants of
what we would call them

00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:44.000
native or Indigenous peoples.

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:50.150
In ordinary American places
over the Midwest and the South,

00:01:50.150 --> 00:01:55.490
we see remnants in the West
of the cliff dwelling people

00:01:55.490 --> 00:02:02.450
the Anasazi or the Navajo
name, the Pueblo peoples who

00:02:02.450 --> 00:02:06.470
are no longer there but have
left behind these structures.

00:02:06.470 --> 00:02:10.052
There's something
haunting about this.

00:02:10.052 --> 00:02:15.110
These civilizations
that were and have gone.

00:02:15.110 --> 00:02:18.650
It's haunting to think
of the earliest explorers

00:02:18.650 --> 00:02:20.720
before Columbus.

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:26.960
Leif Erikson who tried
to start a colony on what

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:30.620
is today the Canadian
island of Newfoundland.

00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:35.180
He and other Norsemen
were trying their best

00:02:35.180 --> 00:02:38.870
to establish a settlement,
and in a sense,

00:02:38.870 --> 00:02:41.150
they could be called the
beginning of American history

00:02:41.150 --> 00:02:41.870
but not really.

00:02:41.870 --> 00:02:45.060
They're more like a false
start on American history,

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:50.660
and yet, they point to
the presence of America

00:02:50.660 --> 00:02:54.380
in the world's imagination
even before America

00:02:54.380 --> 00:02:59.585
was settled as an idea, the
presence of America as an idea.

00:02:59.585 --> 00:03:03.140
A land of hope,
a land of refuge,

00:03:03.140 --> 00:03:07.250
a land of opportunity of
that second chance at life

00:03:07.250 --> 00:03:10.610
for those willing to take it.

00:03:10.610 --> 00:03:13.610
It may seem a little bit
fanciful to attribute

00:03:13.610 --> 00:03:20.510
that motive to the Asiatic,
the first Asiatic immigrants

00:03:20.510 --> 00:03:23.510
to the Western hemisphere.

00:03:23.510 --> 00:03:25.670
We don't know anything
about what they were doing.

00:03:25.670 --> 00:03:29.930
They didn't leave any
remnants to indicate

00:03:29.930 --> 00:03:32.930
one way or the other
what they had in mind.

00:03:32.930 --> 00:03:35.930
We do know that the Norse
explorers were motivated

00:03:35.930 --> 00:03:42.110
by an impulse to explore, to
find new lands and settle them.

00:03:42.110 --> 00:03:45.500
So they were drawn by something
more than just necessity.

00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:52.550
They were drawn by this prospect
of a new world, a new start,

00:03:52.550 --> 00:03:57.080
and this was true 1,000 years
after Christ and 500 years

00:03:57.080 --> 00:03:58.760
before Columbus.

00:03:58.760 --> 00:04:02.330
There was already a
mystique about the West.

00:04:02.330 --> 00:04:05.190
It was in the
literature of antiquity.

00:04:05.190 --> 00:04:10.490
The ancient Greeks spoke
of the Isle of the blessed.

00:04:10.490 --> 00:04:13.580
Homer located the Elysian
fields in the West.

00:04:13.580 --> 00:04:19.100
That was where the stream
of the world's seas flowed.

00:04:19.100 --> 00:04:22.730
Centuries later, Thomas More's
Utopia, Francis Bacon's New

00:04:22.730 --> 00:04:28.820
Atlantis, these are all
expressions of this same theme,

00:04:28.820 --> 00:04:32.580
the promise of the West.

00:04:32.580 --> 00:04:36.300
So it's been thought of for a
long time as a land of plenty,

00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:39.780
land of wealth, a land
of hope, and anticipation

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:44.010
of what a new world can be.

00:04:44.010 --> 00:04:45.460
SPEAKER 2: Thank
you for watching.

